#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2371a64-5e91-5caa-91c0-f6c7ff572c70" :knowledge-point/title "Rotating an Object About the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e99f808d-6060-5342-879e-d4a4e381c5e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333700} {:answer/id #uuid "791a80f3-b4ad-5691-901c-0bb65e7a1492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333701} {:answer/id #uuid "47502db8-ed28-5b5c-a072-895326450cd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333702} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f47db4-7116-59e8-a7f8-fa7e9a9b174c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333703} {:answer/id #uuid "e77e1f3b-2d33-5b19-910d-55243a12ea55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "791a80f3-b4ad-5691-901c-0bb65e7a1492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333701} :answer-field/id #uuid "45a7c453-076f-576e-940b-5e40f3034f85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "723db70a-8ea5-5f25-a3b3-7a3cf5360417" :question/math-academy-id "q-11967" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-1),(-1,2),(2,-3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under a **clockwise** rotation of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(0,\\sqrt{2})$\n- $(3,-1)$\n- $(-5,1)$" :question/worked-solution "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the transformation, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the rotation $R$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are \n\n$$\n(0,\\sqrt{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea036e3-19cd-572b-a6cb-2fa466c2a399" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting a Given Matrix as a Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06ca6946-58ab-5697-81d1-2a70efd22d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians clockwise about the origin." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333757} {:answer/id #uuid "178084ef-c08c-5e37-974b-16b98f4df741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rotation of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333758} {:answer/id #uuid "ea8f4870-ba80-568c-973b-1cb700d3d6ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians clockwise about the origin." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333759} {:answer/id #uuid "38a159b0-c3b2-5948-b173-08544268d828" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333760} {:answer/id #uuid "29c2b12c-de2c-50a4-ab76-962536b40632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29c2b12c-de2c-50a4-ab76-962536b40632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333761} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1c26584-3911-5503-aae6-d9cb1dad3817" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98060d82-cef2-56fd-aec8-1985c72a9485" :question/math-academy-id "q-82050" :question/problem "What is the geometrical interpretation of the transformation represented by the matrix $A$ given below? \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\n{R}_{\\theta }=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComparing $A$ with the rotation matrix, we have \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ are positive, we must have $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nDividing the first equation by the second gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians anticlockwise about the origin."}]}
